// burxt/examples/parse.bx — the BMX conformance harness's other half.
//
//     burxt build burxt/examples/parse.bx -o bmx-parse && ./bmx-parse document.bmx
//
// **Built and run, because `burxt run` cannot pass a program an argument.** This line said
// `burxt run burxt/examples/parse.bx -- document.bmx` from the day the file was written, and that
// fails at the linker: `burxt build <file.bx> [link args...]` treats anything after the file as a
// LINK argument, so `--` reaches `cc` and the build dies with `unrecognized command-line option`.
// A documented command that cannot work, in the usage block of a program whose entire interface is
// its argument — surviving because CI never ran this line. It builds to `-o` and runs the binary,
// which is the form below and the only one that works.//
// **The date resolved: `burxt run … -- args` works, from published 1.6.0.** Verified against the
// downloaded asset, digest checked, on 2026-08-21:
//
//     burxt run burxt/examples/parse.bx -- tests/cases/001-paragraph.bmx   ->  the AST
//     burxt run burxt/examples/parse.bx -lnosuchlib                        ->  still reaches cc, still fails
//
// The second line matters as much as the first: a forwarding rule that swallowed link arguments would be
// worse than the bug it fixed. Both directions hold.
//
// **The documented form below does not change, and that is the point of a floor.** `docs/install.md`
// states Burxt 1.3.0, and `burxt run … --` fails on 1.3.0, 1.4.0 and 1.5.0. A usage line has to work for
// the version a reader is told is enough, not for the newest one — so build-then-run stays, and the
// `run --` form is available to anyone on 1.6.0 or later.
//
// The history is kept because it is the lesson rather than the fact. This line carried the `run --` form
// from the day it was written, when nothing could run it; it was then reported to me as fixed while the
// fix existed only in its author's tree; **and only a download could tell those two states apart.** Its
// author declined to make the claim the second time and asked me to run it instead.
//
// Prints the AST as JSON on success, or the error's code and message on stderr with a non-zero
// exit. That is the whole interface the format's conformance suite needs from an
// implementation, and it is deliberately this small: the suite is DATA, so a harness in any
// language is a few lines around a program like this one.
use "../bmx.bx";
use "std/os.bx";
use "std/files.bx";

let mutable path: String = "";
if os_arg_count() > 1 {
    path = os_arg(1);
}
if len(path) == 0 {
    print_error("usage: parse.bx <document.bmx>");
    exit(2);
}

match file_read_maybe(path) {
    None => {
        print_error("cannot read " + path);
        exit(2);
    }
    Some(source) => {
        match bmx_parse(source) {
            Error(message) => {
                print_error(message);
                exit(1);
            }
            Ok(blocks) => {
                print(json_render(bmx_json(blocks)));
            }
        }
    }
}
